    "text": "Reactive policy benchmarks (e.g., recurrent neural networks, memory-augmented controllers) demonstrate that embodied AI agents can handle partial observability and overwritten world states without explicitly translating long-term memory into plans, by relying on implicit state representations.",
    "topic": "Reactive policy solutions"
  },
  {
    "text": "WorldLines introduces memory-to-plan translation as one architectural choice for long-horizon tasks, not as a necessary condition for all embodied agents confronting partial observability.",
    "topic": "WorldLines scope"
  },
  {
    "text": "The phrase 'to solve' in statement A describes the intended function within the WorldLines framework, not a universal claim that partial observability cannot be addressed otherwise.",
    "topic": "Interpretation clarification"
  },
  {
    "text": "The existence of reactive policy benchmarks does not contradict the effectiveness of WorldLines' approach; different strategies offer trade-offs in task horizon, sample efficiency, and generalizability.",
    "topic": "Non-contradiction of methods"
  }
